            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"Chaos, death, the abyss, and hostile cosmic forces that oppose God; a realm of restless evil and danger.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"Genesis 1:2\",\n    \"Job 38:8-11\",\n    \"Psalm 74:13\",\n    \"Psalm 89:9\",\n    \"Isaiah 57:20\",\n    \"Revelation 13:1\",\n    \"Revelation 21:1\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"The Gentile nations, unregenerate humanity, or multitudes of people in turmoil.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Isaiah 17:12-13\",\n        \"Revelation 17:15\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Judgment, cleansing, and salvation through baptism (passing safely through the waters of death).\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Exodus 14:21-22\",\n        \"1 Corinthians 10:1-2\",\n        \"1 Peter 3:20-21\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"Life, the Holy Spirit, and divine provision (specifically when framed as 'living waters' or rivers, in contrast to the salty, chaotic sea).\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Ezekiel 47:1-12\",\n        \"John 7:37-39\",\n        \"Revelation 22:1-2\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": true,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"In biblical and Ancient Near Eastern symbolism, the 'sea' (Yam) almost universally represents chaos, the abyss, and forces hostile to divine order, which God subdues. This culminates in Revelation 21:1, where the sea is no more. 'Waters' more broadly have a dual symbolism: standing or raging waters represent death and judgment (the Flood, the Red Sea), while flowing 'living waters' represent the Holy Spirit, eternal life, and purification.\"\n  }",
